Prosody glossary reference sources (CC BY 4.0)

The C.Walts prosody glossary (corpus/raw/glossary/prosody_glossary.md) is
owner-authored text. Its definitions are grounded in the open-access works
below, each retrieved and license-verified 2026-08-01 by reading the JATS
<license> element out of the retrieved article itself. Archived copies and
their SHA-256 checksums are under docs/evidence/source-snapshots/; the full
audit, including refused and quarantined candidates, is in
config/glossary_sources.yaml.

All seven are licensed CC BY 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/),
which permits commercial use, redistribution, and adaptation provided the
original author and source are credited. Credit is given here as required.

  Hualde, J. I., Prieto, P., et al. (2016). Towards an International Prosodic
    Alphabet (IPrA). Laboratory Phonology, Ubiquity Press.
    doi:10.5334/labphon.11

  Cole, J., & Shattuck-Hufnagel, S. (2016). New Methods for Prosodic
    Transcription: Capturing Variability as a Source of Information.
    Laboratory Phonology, Ubiquity Press. doi:10.5334/labphon.29

  D'Imperio, M., Cangemi, F., Grice, M., et al. (2016). Introducing Advancing
    Prosodic Transcription. Laboratory Phonology, Ubiquity Press.
    doi:10.5334/labphon.32

  Jeon, H.-S., & Nolan, F. (2017). Prosodic Marking of Narrow Focus in Seoul
    Korean. Laboratory Phonology, Ubiquity Press. doi:10.5334/labphon.48

  Biron, T., Baum, D., Freche, D., et al. (2021). Automatic detection of
    prosodic boundaries in spontaneous speech. PLoS ONE, PLOS.
    doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0250969

  Liu, X., Chen, S., Kuang, J., et al. (2025). Cross-Linguistic Influences on
    L2 Prosody Perception. Brain Sciences, MDPI. doi:10.3390/brainsci15091000

  Powell, S. J., Nayak, S., Magne, C., et al. (2024). Examining the Neural
    Markers of Speech Rhythm in Silent Reading. Brain Sciences, MDPI.
    doi:10.3390/brainsci14111142

